WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:06.240
Okay, friends, another use case for the case statements.

00:06.240 --> 00:11.920
We can use it in order to map values, so we can use the case statement in order to transform the data

00:11.920 --> 00:17.080
from one form to another, in order to make it more readable and more usable for analytics.

00:17.080 --> 00:22.640
One scenario of mapping values is that sometimes the database developers stores the data and values

00:22.640 --> 00:25.920
inside the database as codes and as flags.

00:25.920 --> 00:31.880
So, for example, the status of the order could be stored as one and zero instead of having inactive

00:31.880 --> 00:32.560
and active.

00:32.600 --> 00:37.400
And this is one technique in order to optimize the performance of the database for the application.

00:37.400 --> 00:41.120
Because one and zero is way faster than storing the whole string.

00:41.120 --> 00:45.920
But in that analysis, we usually generate a report to be read by human, by persons.

00:46.240 --> 00:51.280
And now instead of showing the data as zero and one, it's going to be more nicer and readable if you

00:51.320 --> 00:53.800
show the data as active and inactive.

00:53.800 --> 00:59.080
So for this scenarios we're going to go and use the case statement in order to translate those critical

00:59.080 --> 01:02.030
and technical values into readable terms.

01:02.070 --> 01:04.110
Otherwise, each one can consume your report.

01:04.150 --> 01:06.910
Going to ask you what do you mean with the zero and one?

01:06.910 --> 01:12.950
Let's have the following task and it says retrieve employee details with gender displayed as full text.

01:12.990 --> 01:14.550
Okay, so now let's go and solve it.

01:14.550 --> 01:17.110
First we're going to go and explore few informations.

01:17.110 --> 01:19.350
So let's go and show the employee ID.

01:20.510 --> 01:23.910
And let's take the first name last name.

01:24.230 --> 01:26.310
And we need the gender informations.

01:26.310 --> 01:29.910
So gender from sales employees.

01:30.190 --> 01:31.390
So that's it.

01:31.390 --> 01:32.830
Let's go and execute it.

01:32.830 --> 01:35.670
So now as you can see in the results we got our five employees.

01:35.670 --> 01:40.510
And now the gender informations are stored as only one character F and m.

01:40.550 --> 01:44.350
And of course it's easy to understand that the f is female and M is male.

01:44.630 --> 01:47.750
But we would like to show it in the report as a full text.

01:47.750 --> 01:51.590
So female and male instead of those abbreviations.

01:51.590 --> 01:56.430
So in order to do that we're going to go and use the case statement in order to do the mapping between

01:56.430 --> 01:58.430
the old value and the new value.

01:58.460 --> 02:01.460
So let's go and create a new column using the case.

02:01.460 --> 02:04.180
So we're going to have here two conditions because we have two values.

02:04.180 --> 02:05.460
Let's start with the first one.

02:05.460 --> 02:06.860
So we're going to have a new line.

02:06.860 --> 02:07.580
And when.

02:07.940 --> 02:13.740
So when the gender equals to f ladies first then female.

02:14.140 --> 02:16.900
And now for the second value it's going to be exactly the same.

02:16.900 --> 02:22.980
When gender equal to m then we're going to have male.

02:23.340 --> 02:25.940
Be careful for the case sensitivity of the values.

02:25.940 --> 02:28.500
So now of course we will not end this without an else.

02:28.500 --> 02:29.300
So else.

02:29.620 --> 02:31.140
Then we can have the default value.

02:31.140 --> 02:33.620
We're going to have the default value not available.

02:33.780 --> 02:35.300
It's better than having nulls.

02:35.460 --> 02:38.340
So what we are missing is the end.

02:38.820 --> 02:40.420
So we're going to have an end over here.

02:40.420 --> 02:44.020
And we're going to call you gender full text.

02:44.220 --> 02:45.300
So that's it.

02:45.300 --> 02:46.820
Let's go and execute it.

02:46.820 --> 02:51.460
Now if you check the results, we have now done the mapping between the old format of the value with

02:51.460 --> 02:52.340
the new format.

02:52.340 --> 02:54.940
So instead of M we have males and females.

02:54.980 --> 02:56.940
And of course we don't have here any nulls.

02:56.940 --> 02:59.490
That's why we don't have a not available in the data.

02:59.490 --> 03:04.650
But if you have huge data, of course you're going to have somewhere a null and then you will get this

03:04.650 --> 03:05.370
default value.

03:05.370 --> 03:10.010
So this is how you can do mapping between values very easily using the case statements.

03:10.050 --> 03:10.330
Okay.

03:10.330 --> 03:12.530
Let's have another task for the mapping use case.

03:12.530 --> 03:17.250
And the task says retrieve employee details with abbreviated country code.

03:17.290 --> 03:22.530
Sometimes as we are generating reports, maybe using power BI or Tableau, we don't have enough spaces

03:22.530 --> 03:25.610
in order to use the full name of values.

03:25.610 --> 03:26.330
So what do we need?

03:26.370 --> 03:27.490
We need abbreviations.

03:27.490 --> 03:29.810
We need short form of the values.

03:29.810 --> 03:36.570
And we can go and use in SQL the case statement in order to map the full value to an abbreviated value.

03:36.570 --> 03:39.090
So it's like the previous example but the way around.

03:39.130 --> 03:39.450
All right.

03:39.450 --> 03:40.530
So now let's go and solve it.

03:40.570 --> 03:43.930
We're going to go and select few details like the customer ID.

03:44.330 --> 03:46.410
Let's take the first name last name.

03:46.810 --> 03:47.730
And what do we need.

03:47.730 --> 03:52.010
We need the country information from sales customers.

03:52.010 --> 03:53.210
So that's it.

03:53.250 --> 03:54.970
Let's go and execute it.

03:55.010 --> 03:59.480
And as you can see we got our five customers and we have the counter formations as a full name.

03:59.480 --> 04:02.840
Now of course for the reports we need abbreviated values from this.

04:02.840 --> 04:07.200
So we're going to go and map those full names of the countries to a short form.

04:07.200 --> 04:11.480
But in real projects you might get big tables where you have thousands and millions of records.

04:11.480 --> 04:13.360
So you cannot just check it like this.

04:13.360 --> 04:18.520
So how I usually do it, I go and retrieve a distinct list of all values from one column.

04:18.520 --> 04:21.280
So I usually go and have a separate query for that.

04:21.280 --> 04:28.280
So we're going to have select distinct country from the table sales customers.

04:28.280 --> 04:31.360
It's just for me to see all the possible values inside the database.

04:31.360 --> 04:35.360
So now you see the second result over here we have only two values Germany and USA.

04:35.400 --> 04:37.960
And then I can go and map the data correctly.

04:37.960 --> 04:43.000
So always if you are mapping data using the case, when you have to understand all the possible values

04:43.000 --> 04:44.520
that you have inside a table.

04:44.520 --> 04:47.160
So let's go and generate this new informations.

04:47.200 --> 04:49.680
Let's start with case and then new line.

04:49.680 --> 04:54.160
When country equal to the first value it's going to be Germany.

04:54.480 --> 04:56.990
Make sure you write it exactly like in the database.

04:57.230 --> 05:00.510
The first character is capital and the rest is small.

05:00.750 --> 05:01.950
So what happened?

05:02.230 --> 05:04.110
We're going to have the abbreviation of Germany.

05:04.110 --> 05:05.150
It's going to be D.

05:05.190 --> 05:05.590
All right.

05:05.590 --> 05:07.270
So this is for the first value.

05:07.270 --> 05:09.070
And then let's move to the second one.

05:09.110 --> 05:12.790
It's going to be country equal to USA.

05:12.830 --> 05:14.110
It's already abbreviated.

05:14.110 --> 05:16.550
But maybe we can get only two characters.

05:17.030 --> 05:19.110
So us like this.

05:19.150 --> 05:20.630
And now let's go and add an else.

05:20.630 --> 05:21.350
It's optional.

05:21.350 --> 05:24.790
But in case that we have nulls in the data or we get a new value.

05:24.950 --> 05:27.150
So else it's not available.

05:27.150 --> 05:27.950
So na.

05:28.230 --> 05:28.950
So that's it.

05:28.950 --> 05:31.110
And never forget about the end.

05:31.390 --> 05:35.310
So end and the name going to be country abbreviation.

05:35.670 --> 05:36.670
So that's it.

05:36.670 --> 05:39.470
Let me just get rid of the other query.

05:39.470 --> 05:40.950
So the mapping is correct.

05:40.950 --> 05:42.230
Let's go and execute it.

05:42.270 --> 05:46.270
And now if you check the results we got a new column called Country abbreviation.

05:46.270 --> 05:51.030
And as you can see now the mapping is working here we have Germany and we have here the E.

05:51.070 --> 05:52.950
And for the USA we have us.

05:53.070 --> 05:57.700
So with that we have solved the task, and we've done the mapping correctly between the old value and

05:57.700 --> 05:58.500
the new value.

06:02.340 --> 06:06.180
All right, friends, now there is a special case for the syntax of the case statements.

06:06.380 --> 06:08.660
If you are using it for mapping values.

06:08.660 --> 06:09.700
So now let's go and check it.

06:09.700 --> 06:13.380
So now let's say that we have a lot of different distinct values inside their country.

06:13.420 --> 06:14.140
Not only two values.

06:14.140 --> 06:15.260
We have a lot of values.

06:15.660 --> 06:19.980
And if you are mapping the values using the case, when you're going to end up always writing the same

06:19.980 --> 06:25.140
thing country equal, Germany, country equal, India, country equal United States, and so on.

06:25.140 --> 06:27.660
So we are always using the column country.

06:27.660 --> 06:33.420
So the conditions over here using always one column and it's always the operator is equal.

06:33.420 --> 06:37.700
So now only for this scenario we have another syntax for the case statements.

06:37.700 --> 06:38.980
And it looks like this.

06:39.500 --> 06:40.940
We start with a keyword case.

06:40.940 --> 06:45.380
But after that immediately we're going to use the column that we want to evaluate.

06:45.380 --> 06:47.500
And here you can use only one column.

06:47.500 --> 06:49.460
You cannot use multiple columns.

06:49.460 --> 06:53.490
So now we are telling SQL we are now evaluating one column with their country.

06:53.650 --> 06:56.210
And then for each condition, we have the following stuff.

06:56.210 --> 06:57.930
We say when Germany.

06:57.930 --> 07:01.650
That means when country is equal to Germany, then D.

07:01.690 --> 07:04.490
So as you can see here, we don't have here the whole condition.

07:04.490 --> 07:08.770
We have only a possible value that you can see inside the country.

07:08.770 --> 07:11.090
So we are saying is the value country.

07:11.370 --> 07:13.810
If it's true then show the E the next one.

07:13.810 --> 07:17.290
Is it India then in United States US and so on.

07:17.290 --> 07:20.650
So we call this syntax a quick form of the case statement.

07:20.650 --> 07:24.170
And on the left side we call it full form of the case statements.

07:24.170 --> 07:30.210
And of course the restriction and limitation using the quick format is that you can use only one column

07:30.210 --> 07:32.930
and it's only for the equal operator.

07:32.930 --> 07:36.690
So that means only for these scenarios you can go and use the quick format.

07:36.850 --> 07:41.690
If things get a little bit complicated, where you have to mix and make complex logic, you cannot use

07:41.690 --> 07:42.570
the quick formats.

07:42.570 --> 07:47.090
So I would say if you are sure that the logic will not get complicated and you can stay always with

07:47.090 --> 07:51.760
the same column, you can go with the quick format, but I would recommend always to go with the full

07:51.760 --> 07:52.280
format.

07:52.280 --> 07:57.800
Because for one simple reason, if you add one small logic, you have to go and rewrite the whole case

07:57.800 --> 08:01.840
statements back to the full format in order to add any small logic.

08:01.840 --> 08:06.560
But of course there is nothing wrong using the quick form in order to do the case statements.

08:06.560 --> 08:11.960
If the logic can stay static and you are sure we are using only one column and we are just doing mapping,

08:11.960 --> 08:13.320
there is no any extra logic.

08:13.360 --> 08:17.480
Okay, so now let's try this quick format for the case statements for the previous example.

08:17.640 --> 08:20.720
So I will just go and copy everything to a new column.

08:21.120 --> 08:22.960
So I'm just going to rename it to two.

08:23.080 --> 08:24.400
And now how are we going to do it.

08:24.400 --> 08:26.440
So it's going to be case.

08:26.440 --> 08:29.000
But this time we're going to write country.

08:29.480 --> 08:33.040
And then inside the wind we will have only the values.

08:33.040 --> 08:34.640
So no need for the condition.

08:35.120 --> 08:36.760
So it's going to be like this.

08:36.800 --> 08:37.920
Let me scroll up.

08:37.920 --> 08:38.440
So that's it.

08:38.440 --> 08:43.120
As you can see it's smaller and quicker than writing the whole condition each time.

08:43.120 --> 08:45.120
So now let's go and execute this.

08:45.160 --> 08:48.360
And as you can see in the result we're going to get identical values.

08:48.360 --> 08:51.000
So now you know one more trick in that case statement.
